  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "As of March 18, 2026, Micheál Martin is the Taoiseach (Prime Minister) of Ireland, having taken office on January 23, 2025. He serves as the head of government, leading the cabinet, appointing government members, and maintaining majority support in the Dáil Éireann. The role is known for directing policy and representing Ireland internationally, such as in annual St. Patrick's Day visits to Washington D.C.. Current Taoiseach: Name: Micheál Martin (since January 23, 2025). Role and Responsibilities: Head of Government: The Taoiseach is the leader of Ireland's central executive authority. Executive Power: They are responsible for nominating other members of the government (ministers) for appointment by the President. Legislative Function: They lead the Dáil Éireann (Irish Parliament) and are responsible for directing government policy. International Representation: They represent Ireland on the international stage, including attending European Council meetings and negotiating with international leaders. Appointment: The Taoiseach is appointed by the President of Ireland upon the nomination of the Dáil Éireann, and must retain the support of a majority in the Dáil. Key Aspects: Etymology: Taoiseach is an Irish word for \"chief\" or \"leader,\" which has been used in this context since the 1937 Constitution of Ireland. Department: The Taoiseach is supported by the Department of the Taoiseach (Roinn an Taoisigh), located in Dublin. Rotation: Recent Irish political history has included a rotation agreement (2020-2025), where the leadership roles alternated between the Fianna Fáil and Fine Gael parties. Salary: While exact current, individual figures can change, the salary for the Taoiseach is generally among the highest in Irish public service. As of recent years, it is frequently reported at roughly €200,000+ per year.",
